{
  "requirement": "`habeas status` and the mdBook requirements index MUST generate their Definition/Evidence Markdown tables through the same renderer. That renderer MUST calculate column widths, align the columns, and display the same evidence-state labels in both outputs. Callers MAY select different definitions and supply different kind-labeled name cells so mdBook can link to detail pages.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is met.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `render_evidence_table` in `src/markdown.rs` (marked `{impl}`) is the single renderer. It takes `(name cell, &str evidence label)` rows. It computes each column width as the widest cell, with the header names \"Definition\" and \"Evidence\" as the minimum. It then left-aligns and pads the header, separator and data rows to those widths.\n- `status_markdown_with_options` in `src/commands.rs` (`{impl}` at the function and at the call site) calls it. It builds name cells as `` `name` (kind) `` and uses `EvidenceState::label()` for the evidence cell. It also selects definitions by glob and by `--all`, `--verbose` and FIXME handling.\n- `IndexPlan::populate` in `src/index.rs` (`{impl}` at the call site) calls the same function. It builds name cells as `` [`name`](href) (kind) `` so mdBook can link to detail pages. It uses the same `EvidenceState::label()`, and it takes every definition that has a detail page.\n- The two callers therefore share the renderer, the width calculation, the alignment and the state labels. They differ only in which definitions they select and in their name cells, which the requirement allows.\n- All the code that implements the requirement carries a reference to it. I found no unmarked partial implementation. The renderer also carries `status_table_alignment`, which specifies the same alignment behaviour.\n\n**Tests**\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` in `src/index.rs` carries a `{test}` reference to this requirement.\n  - It checks the linked name cells in the index.\n  - It checks the evidence labels Valid, Determination false, Invalid and Missing.\n  - It checks that every table row has `|` delimiters at identical positions, which confirms that column widths are computed and the columns aligned.\n- `tests/commands.rs` has `status_tables_align_each_column_to_its_widest_cell`, which cites `status_table_alignment` rather than this requirement. It exercises the same renderer from the status side.\n- No test cites `shared_status_rendering` on the status side. That is a minor weakness. The requirement is structural, and a single shared function guarantees it, so I judge the coverage adequate.",
